# CKA Lab Exercise: Kubernetes Networking (Services, Ingress & Gateway API)

Hands-on training scenario for the Certified Kubernetes Administrator (CKA) certification, covering Services, Headless
services, Ingress controllers, and the Gateway API (with NGINX Gateway Fabric & Caddy reverse proxy).

---

## 1. Overview & Curriculum Objectives

Networking constitutes a major domain (20%) of the CKA exam curriculum. This lab brings together the core networking
primitives and modern architectural patterns:

- **Module 1 (Services & CoreDNS)**: Stable ClusterIP networking, Headless Services (`clusterIP: None`), DNS resolution
  (`<service>.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local`), multi-port NodePort services, and EndpointSlice mapping.
- **Module 2 (Ingresses)**: L7 HTTP/HTTPS traffic routing via standard Ingress resources, host-based routing, and
  path-prefix matching.
- **Module 3 (Gateway API & L4/L7 Routing)**: Role-oriented routing using standard Gateway API CRDs (`GatewayClass`,
  `Gateway`, `HTTPRoute`, `TLSRoute`), NGINX Gateway Fabric, multi-listener configuration (HTTP, HTTPS termination,
  TLS passthrough), complex matching (headers, query parameters, methods, AND/OR logic), canary traffic splitting
  (`weight`), and Caddy TLS edge forwarding.
- **Module 4 (Network Policies & Native Triage)**: Standard CKA NetworkPolicy creation (`podSelector`,
  `namespaceSelector`, `ports`), default-deny ingress isolation, authorized client gating, and database egress
  filtering (Killer.sh Q24), verified via native CLI diagnostic probes, with auxiliary homelab packet drop tracing via
  Cilium Hubble.

> [!NOTE]
> **Advanced Security Hardening in CKS**:
> While standard CKA functional NetworkPolicies are practiced in Module 4, advanced security hardening
> (cloud metadata protection `169.254.169.254/32`, strict zero-trust microsegmentation, and `kubectl-cyclonus`
> matrix analysis) resides in the dedicated CKS training lab under `/home/vagrant/cks/network-policies/`.

